# The law rank/cycle_space.sqlc owes: the layer graph carries an undirected cycle exactly when some
# fusion's parts fail to partition what they compose. One row, the two instruments held against each
# other.
#
# Which cycle, because the tree uses the word both ways
#   Here it is the undirected one, `m - n + c` over the edges symmetrised in
#   rank/graph_measures.sqlc. composition/descent.sqlc means the directed one, SQL:2016's `CYCLE`
#   clause stopping on a layer that repeats within its own path, and its header says a diamond is
#   not one. Both are right about the same diamond: nothing composes itself, and a layer is still
#   reached twice. Unqualified, the word names neither, and this tree has a second graph whose
#   cycle space is not zero.
#
# Two instruments, neither reading the other
#   rank/graph_measures.sqlc counts nodes, edges and components and reports `m - n + c`.
#   checks/jagged_layer.sqlc asks a different question of a different relation: whether any layer
#   arrives at a fusion through two paths. They share no code. An undirected cycle in `F` IS a
#   layer arriving twice under one fold, so the two cannot disagree, and a green agreement here is
#   evidence rather than two sentences agreeing.
#
# Zero versus nonzero, never the two counts
#   The cycle space counts INDEPENDENT cycles; the rule counts VIOLATING FUSIONS, and one cycle can
#   accuse more than one fusion. Holding the numbers equal would pass today and break on the first
#   document where one cycle implicates two fusions, which is a coincidence rather than a law.
#
# Its own non-vacuity is a column, not a comment
#   `0 = 0` is true of an empty graph and of a rule that examined nothing, so the node count, the
#   edge count and the examined count are in `holds`. A graph with no edges has a cycle space of
#   zero for the wrong reason, and this law refuses to call that a pass.
#
# The cut side of the decomposition
#   The edge space is the cut space plus the cycle space, and this model puts one rule in each. A
#   sum round a loop is the cycle condition, which is the unit graph and
#   checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close. A balance at a node is the cut condition, which is the
#   fusion rule. This law is the statement that the composition graph has nothing in its cycle half.
#   Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entries `incidence_subspaces` and `fusion_adds_no_cycle`.
#
# Why a fusion cannot move it
#   A fusion is one new node and one edge per part, so parts drawn from `k` distinct components
#   leave `m - n + c` where it was, and two parts inside one component raise it by one. That is why
#   the rule is about a partition and not about an arity: no legitimate filing moves this dimension.
#
# examples/graphs/main.rs keeps its own assertion
#   It is the only reader that takes these measures as a program rather than as rows, and it names
#   the mutation that moves both instruments together. Removing it would leave this law as the sole
#   witness to its own claim.
-- rank/graph_measures.sqlc's layer row at the corpus scope against checks/jagged_layer.sqlc.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT 'rank/cycle_space' AS subject,
           x.nodes > 0 AND x.edges > 0 AND x.examined > 0
           AND (x.dim = 0) = (x.violations = 0)                                      AS holds,
           format('dim %s over %s node(s), %s edge(s), %s component(s); %s of %s fusion(s) '
                  'examined violate the partition',
                  x.dim, x.nodes, x.edges, x.components, x.violations, x.examined)   AS detail
    FROM (
        SELECT m.cycle_space_dim AS dim, m.n_nodes AS nodes, m.m_edges AS edges,
               m.c_components AS components, j.violations, j.examined
        FROM      (
            :compose(rank/graph_measures.sqlc)
        ) m
        CROSS JOIN (
            SELECT count(*) FILTER (WHERE k.violates)             AS violations,
                   count(*) FILTER (WHERE k.violates IS NOT NULL) AS examined
            FROM ( :compose(checks/jagged_layer.sqlc) ) k
        ) j
        WHERE m.graph = 'layers' AND m.filing IS NULL
    ) x
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'cycle_space'
